think Picked the next crux Lindy-first: the scrubber proved timed GEOMETRY, so the twin is timed TEXT -- an M:SS clock a video player burns into a corner, whose digits are a pure function of the frame index. The one genuinely new primitive is format_timecode: integer division and modulo turning a seconds count into digit bytes, no float, exact at every second.
think Designed the proof to bind pixels to the formatter: paint the expected readout onto a DISTINCT flat background and compare only the opaque badge rectangle byte for byte, so a match proves the digits seen are the digits format_timecode produced (opacity makes the badge depend on the text alone).
think Added a tick invariant against timecode_seconds as the oracle: within a whole second the badge is byte-steady frame to frame, and it changes exactly at each second boundary -- the clock advances when the second does, never between.
think Composed over public APIs only (background verbs, paint_text_panel, both codecs), reusing the mostly-still moving-block background as ground truth; single-stranded, braiding nothing beneath, inventing no storage/codec/palette/glyph/font.
obs GREEN on the first metal run: format_timecode passes every known answer (0->0:00, 1->0:01, 59->0:59, 60->1:00, 65->1:05, 600->10:00, 5999->99:59); the badge pixels match the formatter byte for byte on all 24 frames; 1 tick + 22 steady pairs over 24 frames of 96x48, touching only the 40x13 badge; the reel round-trips byte for byte through both codecs.
obs The gfd1 adaptive encoder honestly chose 0 deltas + 24 keyframes -- the moving block ticks every frame, so a delta costs more than a whole frame -- so gfd1 (8111 bytes) is a hair larger than gfv1 (8087), reported not claimed (REDS #82). The same honest fallback the scrubber and marquee showed.
